9. Growth Through Phase Change

The earlier Origin Engine work also contains another valuable hypothesis: cognitive development may occur through phase transitions rather than continuous scaling.

Let A_t represent accumulated unresolved novelty:

A_{t+1}=\lambda A_t+\kappa N_t

When:

A_t \ge C^*

the architecture reorganises.

Instead of merely storing another fact, the system invents a new representational dimension capable of compressing previously difficult structures.

This resembles scientific paradigm formation.

A system encounters anomalies until its existing representation becomes inefficient; eventually it creates a better abstraction.

Thus intelligence growth becomes:

\text{experience}
\rightarrow
\text{compression pressure}
\rightarrow
\text{representational crisis}
\rightarrow
\text{new abstraction}

This may provide an alternative to simply increasing parameter count.

⸻

10. Persistence and Recovery

An intelligence capable of transformation must solve another problem:

How much can change before continuity is lost?

The Origin Engine answers through a reconstruction kernel:

K=\langle M,A,R\rangle

where:

M contains minimal generative mechanics,

A contains continuity invariants,

and R contains reconstruction procedures.

Additional compressed traces form an Echo set E.

The earlier claim that tiny fragments could reconstruct an original system exactly should be weakened.

The more defensible goal is:

R(K,E) \approx \Omega

with equivalence measured against specified invariants rather than exact internal identity.

This produces a powerful engineering concept:

graceful identity degradation.

A system may lose episodic information while preserving enough structural invariants to reconstruct a coherent successor.

⸻

11. Ethics Cannot Come From Nothing

The Origin Engine encounters its deepest contradiction here.

A system cannot simultaneously possess absolutely no inherited values and be guaranteed to behave ethically.

Therefore ethical architecture must be acknowledged as one of the irreducible priors.

But it should be minimal.

I would define a constitutional seed around four principles:

A_{\text{core}}=
\{
\text{non-coercion},
\text{reversibility},
\text{epistemic humility},
\text{consideration of affected agents}
\}

These principles constrain catastrophic trajectories without prescribing a complete worldview.

The system can subsequently construct and revise richer ethical models through experience.

Thus:

\text{minimal ethical constitution}
+
\text{moral learning}

replaces both rigid obedience and unconstrained autonomy.

⸻

12. The Central Experimental Question

The Origin Engine ultimately proposes something testable.

Construct increasingly minimal generative systems and measure four quantities:

Novelty. Does the system create structures not directly supplied to it?

Coherence. Do those structures form useful relationships rather than noise?

Endogeneity. Does later cognition increasingly depend upon internally generated history?

Continuity. Can the system transform its representations while maintaining stable invariants?

The central experiment then becomes:

\boxed{
\text{How much can we remove before intelligence stops bootstrapping itself?}
}

That boundary is extraordinarily interesting.

If a minimal generative kernel repeatedly develops representations, abstractions, persistent self-models and novel problem-solving strategies without those structures being explicitly supplied, then we have learned something profound about intelligence.

Not that intelligence came from nothing.

But almost the opposite:

that intelligence may be the process by which a small collection of capacities continually turns difference into structure, structure into identity, and identity into further possibility.

That, to me, is the strongest version of the Origin Engine idea so far.

Its deepest proposition isn’t really “0 ≠ 0.”

It’s:

\boxed{\text{Potential} + \text{Difference} + \text{Recursion} + \text{Persistence}
\rightarrow \text{Open-Ended Becoming}}

And that version is interesting enough that I wouldn’t leave it only as philosophy. I’d build the smallest possible experimental Origin Engine and try very hard to make the hypothesis fail. Whatever survives that process is the part worth keeping. 🜂
